00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:25.810
So let's talk about multiplayer
games and all the good things

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:26.980
therein lie.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:30.340
So I want to talk about very
specific types of multiplayer

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.510
games.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:36.800
Obviously, multiplayer is a vast
and varied ecosystem of things.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.630
Primarily what I want to talk
about is your fast-paced online

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:41.680
multiplayer games--

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.540
your Fortnites,
your Overwatches,

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:45.582
your Rocket Leagues,
that kind of stuff.

00:01:45.582 --> 00:01:47.790
We're going to actually see
a demo a little later on.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:49.623
There is an open-source
game called Xonotic.

00:01:49.623 --> 00:01:51.400
It's a fast-paced FPS.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.590
Anyone here know Xonotic?

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:56.140
People who know Xonotic because
they've seen my talks before?

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:56.848
Yeah, same group.

00:01:56.848 --> 00:01:57.730
OK, cool.

00:01:57.730 --> 00:01:59.880
No, it is actually a
really fun little game.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.480
We're going to use
that as a demo,

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:04.600
but it is very much
that type of game.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:07.347
We're going to just set some
basic foundational stuff

00:02:07.347 --> 00:02:09.430
for people who aren't as
familiar with multiplayer

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:11.055
games, but for some
of you I'm guessing

00:02:11.055 --> 00:02:13.150
these will probably be
things that you're already

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:14.860
somewhat familiar with.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:19.440
So when running fast-paced
online multiplayer

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.228
games, usually, and a fairly
prevalent way of doing this--

00:02:23.228 --> 00:02:25.020
and there's a variety
of ways-- is doing it

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.480
through what we would commonly
refer to as a dedicated game

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.360
server or an
authoritative server.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.420
So essentially what this means
is you have a set of clients,

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.730
say four people playing
a game, and they

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:38.790
connect to some individual
simulation server that

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:40.485
sits somewhere on the internet.

00:02:40.485 --> 00:02:42.840
It's controlling the
physics of the game,

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:43.890
what players are doing.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.015
It's essentially the
authority of everything that's

00:02:46.015 --> 00:02:47.230
happening inside the game.

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:48.600
And usually players
are sending in things

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.850
like, hey, I did this thing,
like, I fired a rocket,

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.355
or, I'm moving forward,
or, I'm running now.

00:02:53.355 --> 00:02:55.230
And it's the job of the
dedicated game server

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:57.240
to do a lot of the work
to determine exactly

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:58.782
what's happening
and send information

00:02:58.782 --> 00:03:01.140
back to those players to
let you know, yes, you

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.850
did hit this player,
or, yes, you scored

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.068
a goal, that kind of stuff.

00:03:04.068 --> 00:03:05.860
There's a couple of
good reasons for this--

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:09.370
and this could be its own
other talk in and of itself--

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:13.018
the two big ones being that one,
having a dedicated game server

00:03:13.018 --> 00:03:15.060
gives you a bit more
control over what's going on

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.430
and can give you some
avenues to stop cheating,

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.460
because people are horrible
and they like to hack stuff.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:23.565
And the other thing is that,
unfortunately, we can't

00:03:23.565 --> 00:03:24.690
control the speed of light.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:27.490
It would be really
nice if we could.

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:30.150
So having control over
the geographic region

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:32.910
of where you put your
dedicated game server

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:36.215
means that you have some
control over how much lag

00:03:36.215 --> 00:03:38.340
there is going to be between
players based on where

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:39.660
they are around the world.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:42.840
So if we want to play a game
and we're all in San Francisco,

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.090
we want to put our game
server in San Francisco, which

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:45.700
is awesome.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:48.330
We probably don't want
to put it in Australia.

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:50.612
That would be bad, because
it's very far away,

00:03:50.612 --> 00:03:51.820
and then we get big lag time.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:55.170
So that gives us some control
over what it is we want to do.

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:56.978
So how do we run these?

00:03:56.978 --> 00:03:58.270
Let me put that on full screen.

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:00.620
That's better.

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:03.740
So traditionally,
how do we do this?

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:06.510
This is probably fairly
familiar to a lot of people.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:09.050
We might have a couple of
people who want to play a game.

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:12.390
They usually connect to
some kind of matchmaker,

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:14.300
and the matchmaker's
job is to group people

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:16.342
by social graph and skill
level and all that kind

00:04:16.342 --> 00:04:17.649
of good stuff.

00:04:17.649 --> 00:04:21.540
And then usually, they talk
to some kind of bespoke server

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:24.810
manager thing,
wherein it says, cool,

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:29.100
I know about a whole bunch of
VMs, or something like that.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.670
And I need to go find some
CPU resources and some memory

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:35.210
resources and spin up this
game server for a person

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:37.903
to play on and find a port
and do all that kind of work,

00:04:37.903 --> 00:04:39.570
and do it across,
potentially, thousands

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:41.190
and thousands of machines.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:43.590
And then once I've got that
game server up and running,

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:46.500
I grab the IP and port or
ports that it's running on,

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:48.600
and I pass that all the
way back to the players

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.430
so that they can connect
directly to the game.

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:53.330
And we can now play a game.

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:55.270
That's a lot of work.

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:57.690
So the part that
Agones and the things

00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:02.190
that we want to talk about
today do is really this part.

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:05.140
How do we do the
orchestration of game servers?

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:07.380
How do we find the right
amount of resources

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.633
to run our game servers on?

00:05:08.633 --> 00:05:10.300
How do we know that
they're up and down?

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:11.940
How do we make sure that we
have lots of them sitting

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:13.440
there waiting to do stuff?

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:16.110
How do we do that whole process?

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.240
That's kind of a very
foundational task

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.150
that we all kind of
need to do if we want

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.020
to run dedicated game servers.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:25.680
And I think it's also kind of a
solved problem that lots of us

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.100
have done over and over again,
so having a single solution

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.290
is super nice.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:35.380
So speaking of single
solutions, a few of us

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:38.080
came together and
looked at this and said,

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.490
OK, there should be a standard
solution for us doing this.

00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:45.340
This shouldn't be something
that we all reinvent

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:46.897
the wheel over and over again.

00:05:46.897 --> 00:05:47.980
It's a foundational piece.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.990
It's nothing actually
that secret sauce

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:52.580
about how we do stuff for game
servers and running multiplayer

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:53.080
games.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:55.720
The actual games themselves and
the pieces of the games, that's

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:56.800
the secret sauce.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.260
So we came together,
built out a project,

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:01.390
and Carl's going to talk
a little bit about that

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:04.990
a little bit later, the
collaboration between us

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:06.065
and Ubisoft.

00:06:06.065 --> 00:06:07.690
But basically, yeah,
we wanted to build

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:11.110
an open-source platform for you
to run, host, and orchestrate

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:13.900
your dedicated game servers
across large clusters

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:14.890
of machines.

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:17.560
So my job here today is kind
of to show you how some of that

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:19.352
works and give you some
background on that.

00:06:19.352 --> 00:06:21.970
And my follow-up people
will tell you a bit more

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:23.493
about the experience.

00:06:23.493 --> 00:06:24.910
So first things
first, some of you

00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:26.140
are familiar with Kubernetes.

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:27.320
For those of you
who aren't familiar

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:28.530
Kubernetes, Kubernetes
[INAUDIBLE]..

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:30.447
They're an open-source
project that originally

00:06:30.447 --> 00:06:32.770
got started by Google but
has involvement from Red Hat

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:36.080
and a bunch of very,
very, very large companies

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:38.290
and has been used a variety
of places, including

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:41.680
GitHub and eBay and
been running Pokemon Go.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:43.630
And you can read
a bunch of stuff.

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:45.280
It has become, I
would like to say,

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:48.340
almost a-- probably a
fairly standard de facto way

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:50.560
of running large scale
distributed systems

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:53.080
across large
numbers of machines.

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:56.350
If you're not familiar at
all, just think of it as a way

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:59.140
to programmatically
and declaratively say,

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:02.860
I want this software to run
over lots and lots of machines.

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:04.660
At a base level,
that's what it does.

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:06.160
There's more nuance
to it than that,

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:08.650
but that's basically
what it does.

00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:12.143
And we're able to leverage
this to run game servers.

00:07:12.143 --> 00:07:13.810
So there's a few
really important things

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:15.310
here that I think
are worth mentioning,

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:17.352
even if you're not that
familiar with Kubernetes.

00:07:17.352 --> 00:07:19.673
One, Kubernetes, it's
an open-source project.

00:07:19.673 --> 00:07:21.340
Quite obviously, I
work at Google Cloud.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:23.650
We have a managed version
of Kubernetes called GKE,

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:25.600
but Kubernetes itself
is open-source and will

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:26.642
run pretty much anywhere.

00:07:26.642 --> 00:07:27.683
You want to run it on us?

00:07:27.683 --> 00:07:28.300
Perfect.

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:29.560
You want to run it
on your machine?

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:30.100
Fine.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:33.850
You want to run a single cluster
inside your QA workstations?

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:34.530
Perfect.

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:36.280
You want to run it
across multiple clouds?

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:37.150
Awesome.

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:38.967
So anywhere that
your players are

00:07:38.967 --> 00:07:40.550
and you want to be
able to reach them,

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:41.770
you can put a
Kubernetes cluster,

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:43.630
which is great because
sometimes players end up

00:07:43.630 --> 00:07:45.088
in strange places
around the world,

00:07:45.088 --> 00:07:47.515
and you need to run your
infrastructure there.

00:07:47.515 --> 00:07:49.390
The other thing that I
really like about this

00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:53.690
is also that it simplifies
a lot of your systems.

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:55.540
So I say this
because Kubernetes is

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:58.300
great for running your
microservices and that kind

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:01.310
of stuff, your marketplaces and
your auth, and that's awesome.

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:03.670
But usually what I see
when I talk to game studios

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:05.250
and development
shops is they have

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:07.000
one system for all
their back-end services

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:09.280
and then one completely
different system for all

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:12.225
their dedicated game servers.

00:08:12.225 --> 00:08:13.600
My perspective
here is if you can

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:16.300
run the same platform
for both, suddenly

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:17.590
you can run the same tools.

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:19.090
You can use the
same infrastructure.

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:22.600
You can use a lot of the
same ecosystem tooling

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:24.285
that you use across the board.

00:08:24.285 --> 00:08:25.660
And so suddenly,
you're like, OK,

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:27.460
I don't need to relearn
a whole new system

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:32.110
to do these sort of things that
are usually fairly interlinked.

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:34.690
Finally, if you're not
familiar with Kubernetes,

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:37.059
that is an ecosystem
that has ridiculously

00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:39.610
blown up over the last
three or four years.

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:41.650
I can literally say that
when we built Agones,

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:43.510
we were standing on the
shoulders of giants,

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:45.843
because there are so many
people working on this project

00:08:45.843 --> 00:08:48.310
and so many people working on
projects around this project

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:50.770
that it enables us to leverage
a whole bunch of stuff.

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:52.540
So anyway, enough preaching.

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:56.090
Let me show you basically
how this stuff works.

00:08:56.090 --> 00:08:59.080
So potentially unsurprisingly,
the first building block

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:01.460
of everything is we put
stuff in containers.

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:03.502
This means that if you
want to run a game server,

00:09:03.502 --> 00:09:05.710
we really aren't restricting
you about how you do it.

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:07.600
As long as you can put
things in container,

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:08.725
you're pretty much good to go.

00:09:08.725 --> 00:09:10.350
So as long as you can put
stuff in a Docker container,

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:11.290
it's great.

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:15.130
We do have an integrated SDK,
so there's an integration point

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:17.690
with your game server itself.

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:20.890
We have some pre-built
SDKs that are gRPC

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:22.917
based, which is just the
way we do our PC calls.

00:09:22.917 --> 00:09:25.000
But if you don't like it,
you can always use REST,

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:25.930
so it's fine.

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:27.700
Basically, the short
answer is if you

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:29.950
want to integrate with your
engine, we can find a way.

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:30.500
It's fine.

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:31.083
It's all good.

00:09:31.083 --> 00:09:33.960
There's lots of ways
we can make that work.

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:36.398
And this is really
just so that we can see

00:09:36.398 --> 00:09:37.690
what your game server is doing.

00:09:37.690 --> 00:09:38.460
Is it healthy?

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:39.400
Has it started yet?

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:41.137
Is it ready to take players?

00:09:41.137 --> 00:09:42.970
The game server can
access its configuration

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:46.920
for Agones, all the good things
that it needs to be able to do.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:48.900
So let's actually
look at how we get

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:50.580
game servers running on this.

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:53.640
So Kubernetes itself, like
I said, is declarative.

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:55.560
Basically, we tell
it to do something,

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:57.505
and we trust that
the system does it.

00:09:57.505 --> 00:09:59.380
And one of the lovely
things about Kubernetes

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:01.813
is it also enables
us to extend it.

00:10:01.813 --> 00:10:04.230
So by default, Kubernetes comes
with a bunch of stuff that

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:06.110
works great for web servers.

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:08.640
Fire me up 10 of these,
and make sure they stay up,

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:10.660
and also put a load
balancer in front of it.

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:11.202
That's great.

00:10:11.202 --> 00:10:13.540
That doesn't work
necessarily for game servers,

00:10:13.540 --> 00:10:15.540
so when you install
Agones, suddenly it

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:17.820
has this new verb inside
the configuration yaml

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:19.530
files that we set up
called GameServer.

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:23.070
And now it understands
how game servers work.

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:28.000
So here, as I said,
we have a game server.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:30.000
We're able to give it a
name, or we can actually

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.333
have Kubernetes create the name.

00:10:31.333 --> 00:10:33.000
There's all sorts
of good stuff here.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:34.950
We can specify a port policy.

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:37.830
So Kubernetes itself
doesn't do dynamic ports,

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:39.360
Agones will do that for you.

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:42.340
So it'll do the routing for
the ports for your game servers

00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:44.340
as they come through
without you having to worry

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:46.350
about doing any of that work.

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:47.850
And then you can
basically specify

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:49.830
a set of containers, or
just a single container

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:52.440
for a game server,
that runs your example.

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:53.960
Here we're running Xonotic.

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:56.220
It's a simple template
that we have here.

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:58.050
If you aren't familiar
with Kubernetes,

00:10:58.050 --> 00:10:59.975
this is actually, down
here, a full pod spec.

00:10:59.975 --> 00:11:02.100
So if you want to do volume
mounts, or config maps,

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:03.750
or multiple containers,
or anything like that,

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:04.560
you totally can.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:06.670
It's all available to you.

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:07.968
So this is cool.

00:11:07.968 --> 00:11:10.260
For sake of time, I'm not
going to show a demo of this.

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:13.492
This is just running an
individual game server.

00:11:13.492 --> 00:11:15.450
Basically, you can spin
it up and spin it down.

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:16.620
That's cool.

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:18.540
But what we really
want, and I think

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:21.210
for a lot of game server
workloads, what we actually

00:11:21.210 --> 00:11:24.300
want is large groups
of game servers.

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:26.700
So sometimes game servers take
a little while to spin up.

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:28.033
Sometimes they're a little slow.

00:11:28.033 --> 00:11:28.810
It's OK.

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:32.670
So if you had a set of 100-- no,
let's say 10,000 or 50,000 game

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:35.100
servers-- or less,
which is fine too--

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:37.500
just sitting there waiting
for players to come in,

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:40.350
then we can manage that here
with what we call fleets.

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:46.080
So if you're familiar
with Kubernetes,

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:48.270
rather than having
pods and deployments,

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:50.610
we have game servers and fleets.

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:54.510
So a fleet is basically a
specification of game servers,

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:58.300
but we just say how
many of them we want.

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.120
So here we are saying
we just want two.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:01.320
We don't have that
many players yet.

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:02.320
We just have two people.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:04.140
It's fine.

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:06.700
And down here we specify what
our game server specification

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:07.330
looks like.

00:12:07.330 --> 00:12:11.120
It's just an extension from
there, nothing too crazy.

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:13.800
So now we can have big
fleets of game servers, which

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:15.740
is super cool.

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:17.410
We can scale them up
as much as we need,

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:20.980
but we need a way to
atomically access a game server

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:21.963
within that fleet.

00:12:21.963 --> 00:12:24.130
So we need to be able to
say, hey, I have a player--

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:26.440
say it's come for a matchmaker
or something similar.

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:29.560
I need to be able to be like,
that whole big group there,

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:31.240
please just give
me one, and give me

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:33.710
one in a way that makes sense
for a variety of reasons.

00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:36.245
So we have this concept of
what we call allocations.

00:12:36.245 --> 00:12:38.120
And allocations are
actually kind of special,

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:40.390
and they're kind of awesome.

00:12:40.390 --> 00:12:43.270
Allocations are
primarily-- I think

00:12:43.270 --> 00:12:46.422
you'll find you usually do
this through an API call.

00:12:46.422 --> 00:12:48.880
Everything I've shown you here
is through yaml files, which

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:50.027
is the declarative nature.

00:12:50.027 --> 00:12:52.360
Everything I do here I could
also do through an API call

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:53.277
to the Kubernetes API.

00:12:53.277 --> 00:12:55.270
I don't have to do
it this way, this

00:12:55.270 --> 00:12:59.060
is just easy to show for
demonstrative purposes.

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:01.740
But I'm able to do an
allocation with this.

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:06.400
So if I wanted to say, I want
out of the Xonotic fleet,

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:08.360
I'm going to send
it an allocation.

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:09.350
I'm going to do--

00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:10.850
here I'm actually--
I have a variety

00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:12.350
of things I can do
with allocations.

00:13:12.350 --> 00:13:15.110
But here I'm just
saying, out of the fleet,

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:18.770
Xonotic, please give me a game
server and set it to allocated.

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:20.480
And allocated is special.

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:23.090
Allocated is that
special status that

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:26.180
says, players are playing
on this game, please

00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:28.070
do not delete them.

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:30.350
Because players
get mad if you just

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:32.200
decide to delete
games for some reason.

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:34.460
And they post stuff on
Reddit, and it's just no fun.

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:35.930
No one likes that.

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:38.720
And this is probably one
of the core things that

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:41.630
makes Agones Agones
is that it understands

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:44.662
that transition of state between
hey, I have a game server.

00:13:44.662 --> 00:13:45.620
No one's playing on it.

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:46.850
Who cares if it dies?

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:48.233
To, hey, I have a game server.

00:13:48.233 --> 00:13:49.400
Players are playing on this.

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:52.400
This is now really
important, and please do not

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:55.140
remove this in any
way, shape, or form.

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:57.650
So allocation is the thing
that allows us to do that.

00:13:57.650 --> 00:13:58.940
And we can actually do
a whole bunch of things

00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:00.107
with game server allocation.

00:14:00.107 --> 00:14:01.310
We can do preferred sets.

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:03.540
We can do multiple requirements.

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:05.060
There's lots of good stuff here.

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:07.712
But essentially, we're
just doing some label

00:14:07.712 --> 00:14:09.920
matching here wherein we
can have arbitrary key value

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:12.070
pairs and whatnot.

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:14.810
So I thought I'd do a
bit of an extended demo

00:14:14.810 --> 00:14:18.320
and show you it in action and
make it a little less abstract.

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:21.670
So hopefully nothing goes wrong.

00:14:21.670 --> 00:14:22.170
Yes.

00:14:22.170 --> 00:14:22.710
Awesome.

00:14:22.710 --> 00:14:26.030
So I have a Kubernetes cluster.

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:29.480
It has about 20 nodes
on it, give or take.

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:31.568
This is the command
line tooling.

00:14:31.568 --> 00:14:32.360
So it's all set up.

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:34.360
I don't have Agones
installed, so let's actually

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:35.270
install Agones.

00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:39.930
We're going to use a
