00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:41.990
to install Agones.

00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:43.770
And I'm going to install it--

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:48.750
Agones system.

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:50.710
That should probably
not be there yet.

00:14:50.710 --> 00:14:52.210
And it'll be Agones/Agones.

00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:56.800
So I'm going to install the
production version of that,

00:14:56.800 --> 00:14:58.510
the most recent release.

00:14:58.510 --> 00:14:59.830
And that's pretty much there.

00:14:59.830 --> 00:15:01.955
That's pretty much what I
have to do to install it.

00:15:01.955 --> 00:15:04.230
There's a few different
ways we can do it.

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:10.840
If we want to have
a look, there's

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:13.375
a few components it installs,
a few different things there.

00:15:13.375 --> 00:15:15.250
Here we have the controller
system as well as

00:15:15.250 --> 00:15:17.687
some latency measurement
stuff that we have installed.

00:15:17.687 --> 00:15:18.520
But that's all good.

00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:22.040
That's pretty much what
I had to do to install.

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:24.100
So if I want to
create a fleet, we

00:15:24.100 --> 00:15:26.600
can use the same yaml
file we did previously.

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:29.850
Again, we could do
this through an API.

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:31.935
We could do this a
bunch of different ways.

00:15:31.935 --> 00:15:36.820
So examples xonotic fleet.yaml
and we can enter that here.

00:15:36.820 --> 00:15:38.780
So this is quite cool.

00:15:38.780 --> 00:15:41.620
So we installed Agones and that
installed those extensions we

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:43.850
were talking about previously.

00:15:43.850 --> 00:15:45.520
So now we can
actually say things

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:50.070
like, hey, Kubernetes, show
me the game servers that

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:52.350
are in your system and show
me the details about them.

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:54.767
That's something that Kubernetes
didn't know about before,

00:15:54.767 --> 00:15:57.030
but now it's integrated
directly in our tools.

00:15:57.030 --> 00:15:59.810
They're spinning up on, I
think, some new clusters.

00:15:59.810 --> 00:16:02.550
They'll come up in a minute.

00:16:02.550 --> 00:16:05.180
Of course, it's a
demo so something--

00:16:05.180 --> 00:16:06.618
they're just pulling down.

00:16:06.618 --> 00:16:07.160
That figures.

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:08.560
They'll come up in a minute.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:09.890
But we can see things here.

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:12.200
So we can see what address
and what port they're on.

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:13.140
They're currently scheduled.

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:14.723
They'll kick over
to ready in probably

00:16:14.723 --> 00:16:17.640
about a minute once
