00:20:59.463 --> 00:21:02.308
I'm going to blame it
on the trackpad mostly.

00:21:02.308 --> 00:21:03.800
Come on.

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:04.790
There we go.

00:21:04.790 --> 00:21:06.104
Thank you.

00:21:06.104 --> 00:21:07.146
I'll let someone kill me.

00:21:10.604 --> 00:21:11.592
Where is one of those?

00:21:15.050 --> 00:21:16.640
There we go.

00:21:16.640 --> 00:21:18.940
So we have a few people
coming and playing against me.

00:21:18.940 --> 00:21:21.340
But yeah, this is
running up on Kubernetes.

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:23.360
It's all working
really, really well,

00:21:23.360 --> 00:21:26.270
and we're able to play a
game, which is super nice.

00:21:26.270 --> 00:21:28.478
I'll show you some other
fun things while we're here.

00:21:30.350 --> 00:21:33.000
So you can start to see some
more fun things in the graphs

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:33.080
now.

00:21:33.080 --> 00:21:34.663
They take a little
bit of time, but we

00:21:34.663 --> 00:21:36.890
have 199 already sitting there.

00:21:36.890 --> 00:21:39.680
You see up in the top left-hand
corner that's all green now.

00:21:39.680 --> 00:21:41.388
We can start seeing
some more things here

00:21:41.388 --> 00:21:43.350
about our rate of
stuff coming up,

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:44.600
as well as how many already.

00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:46.888
We also have autpscaling
capabilities,

00:21:46.888 --> 00:21:48.930
which I'm not going to
talk too much about today.

00:21:48.930 --> 00:21:50.098
We have graphs of that.

00:21:50.098 --> 00:21:51.140
All that sort of metrics.

00:21:51.140 --> 00:21:54.880
All the stuff that you just
need to have to run game servers

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:57.230
and make that really
important stuff without you

00:21:57.230 --> 00:22:00.353
having to do the work to write
all this plumbing type of code.

00:22:00.353 --> 00:22:02.520
So there's a lot of really
cool stuff inside Agones.

00:22:02.520 --> 00:22:03.895
So hopefully that
gives you a bit

00:22:03.895 --> 00:22:07.850
of an idea of how that
whole system works.

00:22:07.850 --> 00:22:11.030
We come over here again too.

00:22:11.030 --> 00:22:13.845
We can also have some more
fun because we have some time.

00:22:13.845 --> 00:22:14.970
I can scale this back down.

00:22:14.970 --> 00:22:20.630
So if I say scale
fleet xonotic replicas.

00:22:20.630 --> 00:22:22.640
Say I want to make that zero.

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:24.950
That's going to start
deleting game servers,

00:22:24.950 --> 00:22:27.020
but we have one game
server that's allocated

00:22:27.020 --> 00:22:29.253
and so we don't want
that one to die.

00:22:29.253 --> 00:22:30.920
And so what we will
actually see is yes,

00:22:30.920 --> 00:22:33.450
it will go and get rid of all
the game servers that we would

00:22:33.450 --> 00:22:35.600
have had previously,
but it won't

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:38.090
touch that one game server
where we have players on it.

00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:39.173
So that's super important.

00:22:39.173 --> 00:22:41.135
So even if we are
editing our fleets

00:22:41.135 --> 00:22:43.010
and we have a rolling
update rolling through,

00:22:43.010 --> 00:22:45.170
which is something
we support, or you

00:22:45.170 --> 00:22:47.848
want to scale a fleet to adjust
workloads based on, say, maybe

00:22:47.848 --> 00:22:49.640
you have less players
coming in, you always

00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:51.807
know that your players are
safe on that game server.

00:22:56.227 --> 00:22:57.560
See how quickly that comes down.

00:22:57.560 --> 00:22:58.130
There we go.

00:22:58.130 --> 00:23:01.430
And so we have that
one allocated now.

00:23:01.430 --> 00:23:02.503
That never went away.

00:23:02.503 --> 00:23:03.420
It always stays there.

00:23:03.420 --> 00:23:05.451
That's super important.

00:23:05.451 --> 00:23:07.690
Cool.

00:23:07.690 --> 00:23:11.620
So that was me showing you
how some of that stuff worked.

00:23:11.620 --> 00:23:14.200
That means now that
when you have players

00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:16.420
that want to play a game,
rather than connecting

00:23:16.420 --> 00:23:18.940
to some bespoke
system they can take

00:23:18.940 --> 00:23:20.980
some sort of custom
matchmaker, potentially Open

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:22.370
Match for some strange reason.

00:23:22.370 --> 00:23:24.430
Also a fun project we work on.

00:23:24.430 --> 00:23:26.620
But they can talk directly
to the Kubernetes API.

00:23:26.620 --> 00:23:29.170
They can reuse all the stuff
that Kubernetes' API gives you.

00:23:29.170 --> 00:23:31.330
As soon as we install
Agones the tooling changes,

00:23:31.330 --> 00:23:32.530
the API changes.

00:23:32.530 --> 00:23:34.683
It understands how to
manage all these things.

00:23:34.683 --> 00:23:36.100
And then the
Kubernetes API can be

00:23:36.100 --> 00:23:39.490
like, hey, Agones, I'd really
like a game server to be

00:23:39.490 --> 00:23:40.690
allocated to me please.

00:23:40.690 --> 00:23:42.040
So cheers, thanks.

00:23:42.040 --> 00:23:43.547
Appreciate that.

00:23:43.547 --> 00:23:45.880
And then the information can
be sent back to the players

00:23:45.880 --> 00:23:47.260
and the players can play a game.

00:23:47.260 --> 00:23:49.600
And so you really can just focus
on what that game server is

00:23:49.600 --> 00:23:51.350
doing and how it works,
rather than having

00:23:51.350 --> 00:23:55.080
to worry about how it
spins up and spins down.

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:55.710
All right.

00:23:55.710 --> 00:23:56.373
Enough of me.

00:23:56.373 --> 00:23:58.290
I'd like to introduce
Carl Dionne to come talk

00:23:58.290 --> 00:24:00.721
about how things are
going over at Ubisoft.

00:24:00.721 --> 00:24:04.158
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:06.122 --> 00:24:08.580
CARL DIONNE: Hi.

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:12.515
For some reason, Mark and
I have similar t-shirts.

00:24:12.515 --> 00:24:13.630
So I'm Carl.

00:24:13.630 --> 00:24:16.750
I'm Director of
Development at Ubisoft.

00:24:16.750 --> 00:24:20.560
I'm responsible for the
multiplayer products,

00:24:20.560 --> 00:24:23.850
multiplayer services
in the online group.

00:24:23.850 --> 00:24:28.290
So that's mainly the network
code, matchmaking, as well as

00:24:28.290 --> 00:24:33.120
the server allocation products.

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:35.247
So I work at Ubisoft.

00:24:35.247 --> 00:24:36.330
You probably know Ubisoft.

00:24:36.330 --> 00:24:38.670
We're the third
largest publisher.

00:24:38.670 --> 00:24:40.680
We have a bunch of people.

00:24:40.680 --> 00:24:43.030
14,000 employees
and that's growing,

00:24:43.030 --> 00:24:46.140
so it's a pretty big company.

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:48.850
You probably know our games.

00:24:48.850 --> 00:24:52.080
So today I want to talk a
bit about why we actually

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:56.730
were interested to
collaborate on Agones.

00:24:56.730 --> 00:24:59.790
And let me start
by talking a bit

00:24:59.790 --> 00:25:02.280
about the change that's
happening in the industry

00:25:02.280 --> 00:25:03.820
today.

00:25:03.820 --> 00:25:07.890
If we look back, it's
not that long ago

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:11.980
releasing the game used
to be the objective,

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:15.450
and now it's just
only the beginning.

00:25:15.450 --> 00:25:19.770
Players are expecting to see
their games as a service.

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:21.925
It's not just a box
product anymore.

00:25:21.925 --> 00:25:23.050
They want to see a service.

00:25:23.050 --> 00:25:27.230
They want to see a game that
keeps changing all the time.

00:25:27.230 --> 00:25:30.360
They want to be
surprised by new content

00:25:30.360 --> 00:25:33.840
whenever they log onto the game.

00:25:33.840 --> 00:25:36.420
So that game as a service
trend is something

00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:38.400
that continues and accelerates.

00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:42.040
And I think that when you look
at cloud gaming, for example,

00:25:42.040 --> 00:25:44.640
to me, it's just a
natural extension of this.

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:49.860
That's the way that players
wants to have their game.

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:52.690
So let me talk a little
bit about what impact

00:25:52.690 --> 00:25:54.940
it has on our games.

00:25:54.940 --> 00:25:58.150
So when we look at the game
[INAUDIBLE] the game client.

00:25:58.150 --> 00:25:59.150
So what's a game client?

00:25:59.150 --> 00:26:01.560
Well, it's a thick client.

00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:03.780
It can be pretty
big, like when we

00:26:03.780 --> 00:26:05.740
look at the latest
Assassin's Creed,

00:26:05.740 --> 00:26:10.870
let's say 42 gigs client,
so that's pretty big.

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:15.090
That client is going to connect
to an ecosystem of services

00:26:15.090 --> 00:26:16.120
that will be running.

00:26:16.120 --> 00:26:20.850
So that includes stuff like
matchmaking, challenges, stats,

00:26:20.850 --> 00:26:21.930
whatever.

00:26:21.930 --> 00:26:27.040
And as we are building
rich games as services,

00:26:27.040 --> 00:26:29.340
it means that
ecosystem of services

00:26:29.340 --> 00:26:32.070
is becoming richer and
richer because that's

00:26:32.070 --> 00:26:34.410
one good place where
we can deliver value

00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:37.560
and content to our players.

00:26:37.560 --> 00:26:39.690
One thing that we see
as well is if we're

00:26:39.690 --> 00:26:42.100
going to build
that type of games,

00:26:42.100 --> 00:26:44.400
it means that we
need to have content

00:26:44.400 --> 00:26:49.410
that will be available for
the players for a long time.

00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:51.540
So an easy way to
make a game that

00:26:51.540 --> 00:26:54.510
will have hundreds
of hours of gameplay

00:26:54.510 --> 00:26:56.670
is to make it a
multiplayer game,

00:26:56.670 --> 00:26:59.890
so that's the reason why we
see a lot of multiplayer games

00:26:59.890 --> 00:27:02.490
nowadays.

00:27:02.490 --> 00:27:04.200
And so typically it
means that you're

00:27:04.200 --> 00:27:09.240
going to need to have a
game server to actually host

00:27:09.240 --> 00:27:14.020
that multiplayer application.

00:27:14.020 --> 00:27:18.390
Now that's going to come
with a number of challenges.

00:27:18.390 --> 00:27:22.050
First, we need to be agile.

00:27:22.050 --> 00:27:24.380
We need to be able
to adapt super

00:27:24.380 --> 00:27:26.810
fast to the needs
of the players,

00:27:26.810 --> 00:27:30.930
and that's true across
the entire ecosystem.

00:27:30.930 --> 00:27:35.610
We know how to be fast at
deploying new services,

00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:38.420
but we also need to be able
to do it through the game

00:27:38.420 --> 00:27:40.490
client and the game
servers as well to be

00:27:40.490 --> 00:27:43.250
able to deploy new content.

00:27:43.250 --> 00:27:45.200
Another challenge
that we have, as well,

00:27:45.200 --> 00:27:49.280
is the question of scale and
the question of proximity.

00:27:49.280 --> 00:27:52.430
We want to make sure that
these game servers sit

00:27:52.430 --> 00:27:56.180
very close to the players
because if latency is high

00:27:56.180 --> 00:27:58.580
that's going to have an
impact on the performance

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:02.050
that they see.

00:28:02.050 --> 00:28:04.450
When you think about
scale, game servers

00:28:04.450 --> 00:28:07.140
are pretty CPU intensive.

00:28:07.140 --> 00:28:09.990
To give an idea, at
Ubisoft we currently peak

00:28:09.990 --> 00:28:13.170
at about 100,000
virtual machines

00:28:13.170 --> 00:28:15.850
to run our game servers.

00:28:15.850 --> 00:28:19.920
But when look at that, like
having 100,000 virtual machines

00:28:19.920 --> 00:28:24.680
across dozens of data centers,
that's a pretty big number.

00:28:24.680 --> 00:28:27.960
That's going to be close to the
capacity of many data centers

00:28:27.960 --> 00:28:29.520
that you see.

00:28:29.520 --> 00:28:33.750
So that makes your typical
blue-green deployment

00:28:33.750 --> 00:28:35.790
quite a big challenge.

00:28:39.130 --> 00:28:42.760
So in that context,
making that type of game

00:28:42.760 --> 00:28:45.110
is becoming
increasingly complex,

00:28:45.110 --> 00:28:51.600
and I think that collaboration
is something that is important.

00:28:51.600 --> 00:28:53.380
It's a nice slide, eh?

00:28:53.380 --> 00:28:54.280
It's a bit cheesy.

00:28:54.280 --> 00:28:56.620
I think that we could
make a nice poster,

00:28:56.620 --> 00:29:00.700
like you have these nice
motivational posters.

00:29:00.700 --> 00:29:05.463
But seriously, collaboration
between our teams at Ubisoft,

00:29:05.463 --> 00:29:06.880
collaborations
between the brands,

00:29:06.880 --> 00:29:09.340
and collaborations with
other people in the industry

00:29:09.340 --> 00:29:11.530
or outside of industry,
I think is something

00:29:11.530 --> 00:29:13.240
that's very important.

00:29:13.240 --> 00:29:18.920
And that's basically
how Agones was born.

00:29:18.920 --> 00:29:21.260
So a while back--

00:29:21.260 --> 00:29:28.320
it's about maybe a bit
more than a year ago--

00:29:28.320 --> 00:29:33.410
we knew that Stadia was coming,
and I was talking to Mark.

00:29:33.410 --> 00:29:36.885
And for some reason,
if we look at Stadia

00:29:36.885 --> 00:29:38.885
and we have the game's
client of running on GCP,

00:29:38.885 --> 00:29:41.887
it makes sense to have the
server running in GCP as well.

00:29:41.887 --> 00:29:43.470
And so I was talking
to Mark and said,

00:29:43.470 --> 00:29:49.130
hey, what are Google's plans
regarding game servers?

00:29:49.130 --> 00:29:52.410
And we chatted for
a little while,

00:29:52.410 --> 00:29:56.360
and we realized that we had
a very nice collaboration

00:29:56.360 --> 00:29:59.640
opportunity, where basically
we could say, well,

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:06.290
let's put together the knowledge
of Google for what it means

00:30:06.290 --> 00:30:09.760
to be able to scale and
deploy containers in scale,

00:30:09.760 --> 00:30:12.350
with Ubisoft's expertise of
what it takes to actually

00:30:12.350 --> 00:30:15.620
build and manage game servers.

00:30:15.620 --> 00:30:19.110
And that's basically
how Agones was born.

00:30:19.110 --> 00:30:21.380
So it's an open-source
project that

00:30:21.380 --> 00:30:24.530
makes it possible to deploy
game servers at scale

00:30:24.530 --> 00:30:26.000
across Kubernetes.

00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:32.560
So it actually was a nice
collaboration [INAUDIBLE]..

00:30:32.560 --> 00:30:38.310
So one thing that I really
like is since Agones is based

00:30:38.310 --> 00:30:40.510
on Kubernetes, what do we get?

00:30:40.510 --> 00:30:42.900
Well, we actually have
a lot of flexibility

00:30:42.900 --> 00:30:47.160
because now we can actually
deploy these game servers

00:30:47.160 --> 00:30:50.170
across different
type of clusters.

00:30:50.170 --> 00:30:52.410
So we can go, for example, to--

00:30:52.410 --> 00:30:55.110
we can deploy them
with physical clusters,

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:56.760
and that's a good
way to save the cost.

00:30:56.760 --> 00:30:58.150
And that's important.

00:30:58.150 --> 00:31:00.690
And when we have
a spike, then we

00:31:00.690 --> 00:31:06.685
can actually go on the cloud
to be able to handle the burst.

00:31:09.540 --> 00:31:13.050
So Agones is something
that's part of a wider

00:31:13.050 --> 00:31:17.122
ecosystem at Ubisoft.

00:31:17.122 --> 00:31:19.080
It's part of a [? complete ?]
ecosystem made up

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:22.170
of hundreds of applications
and microservices

00:31:22.170 --> 00:31:25.140
to manage the online
at Ubisoft, and I

00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:28.710
think it was a pretty good
example of collaboration

00:31:28.710 --> 00:31:30.707
between two companies.

00:31:30.707 --> 00:31:32.695
Thanks.

00:31:32.695 --> 00:31:36.174
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:41.650 --> 00:31:42.680
EDWARD CHIN: Hello.

00:31:42.680 --> 00:31:43.720
Hi, I'm Ed.

00:31:43.720 --> 00:31:46.690
I'm one of the
