00:31:59.620 --> 00:32:01.850
that we have in development.

00:32:01.850 --> 00:32:04.420
I'm going to go
through why we decided

00:32:04.420 --> 00:32:07.420
to use Agones, how
we're using it,

00:32:07.420 --> 00:32:12.530
and also a bit about our
experience with it so far.

00:32:12.530 --> 00:32:14.780
So quickly, first a
bit of background.

00:32:14.780 --> 00:32:17.890
Supersolid, we're a
team of about 40 people

00:32:17.890 --> 00:32:20.810
who are based in
London in the UK,

00:32:20.810 --> 00:32:24.120
and so far we have
these five games.

00:32:24.120 --> 00:32:27.680
So our first two games, Super
Penguins and Pororo Penguin Run

00:32:27.680 --> 00:32:29.510
are both infinite runners.

00:32:29.510 --> 00:32:33.800
We've got Adventure Town, which
is a town building light RPG

00:32:33.800 --> 00:32:36.620
game, and then our two
most recent games are

00:32:36.620 --> 00:32:38.210
Food Street and Home Street.

00:32:38.210 --> 00:32:41.560
They're resource management
simulation games.

00:32:41.560 --> 00:32:43.850
As you probably
noticed, none of these

00:32:43.850 --> 00:32:46.820
are multiplayer
games, or at least not

00:32:46.820 --> 00:32:49.070
real-time, synchronous
multiplayer games,

00:32:49.070 --> 00:32:52.880
but that is what we currently
have in development.

00:32:52.880 --> 00:32:56.510
I can show you a bit
more about this game.

00:32:56.510 --> 00:33:00.260
It's called Snake
Rivals and here is

00:33:00.260 --> 00:33:07.360
a short teaser, which I might
need Mark to play for me.

00:33:18.621 --> 00:33:19.288
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:33:19.288 --> 00:33:22.767
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:33:52.090 --> 00:33:54.575
[END PLAYBACK]

00:33:54.575 --> 00:33:58.054
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:18.030 --> 00:34:20.780
Great, thanks for that, Mark.

00:34:20.780 --> 00:34:21.480
Thanks.

00:34:21.480 --> 00:34:23.340
So that's all our latest game.

00:34:23.340 --> 00:34:25.270
It's out later this year.

00:34:25.270 --> 00:34:26.460
It's called Snake Rivals.

00:34:26.460 --> 00:34:30.300
It's our first real-time,
synchronous multiplayer game.

00:34:30.300 --> 00:34:34.020
The idea is that there are
50 players all in an arena

00:34:34.020 --> 00:34:37.449
at once, and we've got
these three different modes.

00:34:37.449 --> 00:34:40.780
There's a classic mode, which is
kind of similar to an IO game,

00:34:40.780 --> 00:34:42.239
if you like.

00:34:42.239 --> 00:34:46.530
So players come in and the
session ends when they die.

00:34:46.530 --> 00:34:48.030
And then we've got
two other modes--

00:34:48.030 --> 00:34:51.600
a gold rush mode and
a battle royale mode.

00:34:51.600 --> 00:34:57.450
In gold rush it's more timed,
so in more strategic game play

00:34:57.450 --> 00:35:00.370
you're trying to make kills
or steal kills, and then

00:35:00.370 --> 00:35:03.990
at the end the player who
has the most points wins.

00:35:03.990 --> 00:35:05.970
And the third mode,
battle royale,

00:35:05.970 --> 00:35:09.060
I am assuming that everyone
knows what that is.

00:35:09.060 --> 00:35:12.075
And basically, the last
surviving player wins.

00:35:14.820 --> 00:35:18.600
So about I guess 12 months
ago, a little less than that,

00:35:18.600 --> 00:35:20.550
we decided to make this game.

00:35:20.550 --> 00:35:22.220
The first question
I guess was, well,

00:35:22.220 --> 00:35:24.240
how are we going to build it?

00:35:24.240 --> 00:35:27.030
And quite quickly we
decided to use Kubernetes,

00:35:27.030 --> 00:35:30.180
so the big question
for early research

00:35:30.180 --> 00:35:35.070
was what implementation of
Kubernetes we wanted to use.

00:35:35.070 --> 00:35:40.010
We settled on GKE, which
is Google's implementation

00:35:40.010 --> 00:35:41.570
of Kubernetes.

00:35:41.570 --> 00:35:43.380
We did that for a few reasons.

00:35:43.380 --> 00:35:45.200
I think we basically
tried it out

00:35:45.200 --> 00:35:47.690
on a few different services.

00:35:47.690 --> 00:35:50.240
With GKE we found it
was just the easiest

00:35:50.240 --> 00:35:52.450
to set up and get running.

00:35:52.450 --> 00:35:56.140
There were also a few other
advantages that we liked.

00:35:56.140 --> 00:36:00.200
The ease of using
the GCP services,

00:36:00.200 --> 00:36:02.770
such as load balancing as well.

00:36:02.770 --> 00:36:05.320
That was pretty important to us.

00:36:05.320 --> 00:36:09.490
Debugging live code, easy error
tracking of live incidents,

00:36:09.490 --> 00:36:11.830
and also we found it's
also relatively cost

00:36:11.830 --> 00:36:15.190
effective, which
is also something

00:36:15.190 --> 00:36:17.960
important to consider.

00:36:17.960 --> 00:36:22.720
So having decided to use
GKE, we found that we also

00:36:22.720 --> 00:36:24.910
had three key requirements.

00:36:24.910 --> 00:36:27.690
This is stuff that I guess
Mark went through earlier,

00:36:27.690 --> 00:36:31.340
but it's pretty important
for these multiplayer games.

00:36:31.340 --> 00:36:34.270
So the first thing
is that we've got

00:36:34.270 --> 00:36:37.570
to be able to start up
game servers on demand.

00:36:37.570 --> 00:36:41.420
So in the endless
mode, for example,

00:36:41.420 --> 00:36:43.510
when the arena fills
up with 50 players

00:36:43.510 --> 00:36:45.020
we need to start a new arena.

00:36:45.020 --> 00:36:47.610
So we need to be able to
start a new game server.

00:36:47.610 --> 00:36:49.210
And for the other
modes, when players

00:36:49.210 --> 00:36:51.580
want to start a
new game, again, we

00:36:51.580 --> 00:36:53.830
need to start up game
servers on demand.

00:36:53.830 --> 00:36:56.980
And when the games are
over, when they're finished,

00:36:56.980 --> 00:37:00.150
we also need to be
able to shut them down.

00:37:00.150 --> 00:37:05.220
The second requirement, which I
think Mark touched on as well,

00:37:05.220 --> 00:37:08.910
is to be able to protect running
servers from shutting down.

00:37:08.910 --> 00:37:12.160
So this is pretty key.

00:37:12.160 --> 00:37:15.160
So if a service gets
updated, for example,

00:37:15.160 --> 00:37:17.140
you don't want
your games servers

00:37:17.140 --> 00:37:19.690
to shut down because everyone--

00:37:19.690 --> 00:37:22.140
all your players
currently playing--

00:37:22.140 --> 00:37:24.030
will get kicked off their game.

00:37:24.030 --> 00:37:27.330
And then your Google
Play rating goes down,

00:37:27.330 --> 00:37:30.780
and that's always
a terrible thing.

00:37:30.780 --> 00:37:33.180
Then the third
requirement is, of course,

00:37:33.180 --> 00:37:34.570
the scale based on demand.

00:37:34.570 --> 00:37:39.880
So number of players and
their location as well.

00:37:39.880 --> 00:37:42.870
And what we found
was Agones just

00:37:42.870 --> 00:37:47.070
helps solve all of
these issues for us,

00:37:47.070 --> 00:37:50.620
and that's the main reason
why we decided to use Agones.

00:37:53.150 --> 00:37:57.030
Moving on to exactly
how we're using Agones.

00:37:57.030 --> 00:38:02.420
So this is what's going
on with Snake Rivals.

00:38:02.420 --> 00:38:07.130
So when you start the game, the
client asks for the game mode.

00:38:07.130 --> 00:38:11.240
So whatever game mode
the player asks for.

00:38:11.240 --> 00:38:15.935
That then goes to the Google
Global Load Balancer--

00:38:15.935 --> 00:38:18.140
not the easiest thing to say--

00:38:18.140 --> 00:38:20.240
and that asks for
the matchmaker.

00:38:20.240 --> 00:38:21.710
Also, it selects
the best cluster

00:38:21.710 --> 00:38:24.930
for the client to connect to.

00:38:24.930 --> 00:38:29.050
The matchmaker then finds
that specific game mode,

00:38:29.050 --> 00:38:33.930
a game for that to join,
and Kubernetes via REST API

00:38:33.930 --> 00:38:37.080
gets the list of game servers.

00:38:37.080 --> 00:38:39.450
And that's managed by
Agones, which maintains

00:38:39.450 --> 00:38:41.990
the pool of game servers.

00:38:41.990 --> 00:38:46.290
The matchmaker then decides
from the list of game servers.

00:38:46.290 --> 00:38:50.870
And again, via REST
API with Agones,

00:38:50.870 --> 00:38:52.685
we mark that
selected game server

00:38:52.685 --> 00:38:57.650
as allocated so that importantly
it doesn't get destroyed.

00:38:57.650 --> 00:38:59.600
The matchmaker then
returns that one game

00:38:59.600 --> 00:39:02.060
server to the
client and the game

00:39:02.060 --> 00:39:04.850
starts and the
player obviously has

00:39:04.850 --> 00:39:06.410
a great time playing the game.

00:39:06.410 --> 00:39:11.030
And when that game finishes,
again via REST API and Agones,

00:39:11.030 --> 00:39:15.780
we then mark that
server as shutting down.

00:39:15.780 --> 00:39:19.860
And that's at a
pretty high level

00:39:19.860 --> 00:39:21.925
how we're using GKE and Agones.

00:39:24.780 --> 00:39:30.730
And so finally, it's a question
on how we're finding it,

00:39:30.730 --> 00:39:34.750
and it's been
pretty great so far.

00:39:34.750 --> 00:39:40.460
We've seen that it's a pretty
supportive environment.

00:39:40.460 --> 00:39:45.190
So there's a Slack group
that's pretty responsive.

00:39:45.190 --> 00:39:46.180
There's a Google Group.

00:39:46.180 --> 00:39:49.360
There are community meetings
that happen quite regularly.

00:39:49.360 --> 00:39:52.210
It allows us to
influence the roadmap

00:39:52.210 --> 00:39:56.540
of what's being produced,
prioritization of that as well.

00:39:56.540 --> 00:40:00.070
And then obviously we're able
to report any bugs that we come

00:40:00.070 --> 00:40:02.970
across, so that's been great.

00:40:02.970 --> 00:40:06.650
We've also, through this,
built a great relationship

00:40:06.650 --> 00:40:09.320
with Google, and that's
given us great access

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:16.220
to training materials,
so courses and Kubernetes

00:40:16.220 --> 00:40:20.720
workshops as well, which has
been really positively received

00:40:20.720 --> 00:40:22.440
by our developers.

00:40:22.440 --> 00:40:24.390
So thanks for that.

00:40:24.390 --> 00:40:27.290
We've also had access to a
production readiness review,

00:40:27.290 --> 00:40:29.520
and that's been really useful.

00:40:29.520 --> 00:40:32.390
So they assess the
game architecture,

00:40:32.390 --> 00:40:40.220
and they give suggestions
on how to operate

00:40:40.220 --> 00:40:41.710
at Google's standards.

00:40:41.710 --> 00:40:44.410
That's the SRE methodology.

00:40:44.410 --> 00:40:46.340
All of this is just
super important to us

00:40:46.340 --> 00:40:49.270
because I think all
game developers here

00:40:49.270 --> 00:40:53.108
just know how hard it is
to develop a hit game.

00:40:53.108 --> 00:40:55.150
And once you have that
you just want to make sure

00:40:55.150 --> 00:40:58.240
that everything scales
properly and all

00:40:58.240 --> 00:41:04.040
your game servers
are up and running.

00:41:04.040 --> 00:41:09.170
Finally, we probably have
to say something negative,

00:41:09.170 --> 00:41:12.020
if there is anything
negative at all.

00:41:12.020 --> 00:41:16.170
The only thing is to point
out it's an alpha service

00:41:16.170 --> 00:41:21.090
so you might get what you
expect from alpha services.

00:41:21.090 --> 00:41:25.730
If a feature gets deprecated you
might have to code around that,

00:41:25.730 --> 00:41:27.570
or you might be working
away at a feature

00:41:27.570 --> 00:41:30.440
and then you realize
that actually that's

00:41:30.440 --> 00:41:33.050
what they're building next.

00:41:33.050 --> 00:41:35.060
But you know what?

00:41:35.060 --> 00:41:37.110
It really doesn't matter to us.

00:41:37.110 --> 00:41:39.080
The communication
has been great so we

00:41:39.080 --> 00:41:45.700
avoid a lot of these issues,
and also if we weren't using it

00:41:45.700 --> 00:41:48.070
we'd really have to build
it ourselves anyway.

00:41:48.070 --> 00:41:53.370
So for us it really
has been a no brainer.

00:41:53.370 --> 00:41:55.010
So to sum up--

00:41:55.010 --> 00:42:00.530
with GCP, with GKE,
Agones, it's really

00:42:00.530 --> 00:42:05.000
allowed us to be able to build
Snake Rivals with a relatively

00:42:05.000 --> 00:42:11.590
small team, probably just
two server developers,

00:42:11.590 --> 00:42:13.920
in under 12 months.

00:42:13.920 --> 00:42:17.930
So that's been great.

00:42:17.930 --> 00:42:21.330
Thanks, and look out for Snake
Rivals coming later this year.

00:42:21.330 --> 00:42:22.280
Thanks.

