set any hard limits?

00:47:31.160 --> 00:47:32.450
Not really, no.

00:47:32.450 --> 00:47:35.630
As your Docker containers get
larger they can be unwieldy.

00:47:35.630 --> 00:47:37.460
There are some
strategies we do have

00:47:37.460 --> 00:47:39.293
that we're starting to
talk about internally

00:47:39.293 --> 00:47:41.197
about how to share
assets between containers

00:47:41.197 --> 00:47:42.530
and how to load them up quickly.

00:47:42.530 --> 00:47:44.960
We can do some in-memory
mapping type things.

00:47:44.960 --> 00:47:47.690
That's another area we want to
explore as we go forward too.

00:47:47.690 --> 00:47:48.680
A lot of people in
development just

00:47:48.680 --> 00:47:51.170
build really big containers,
like 3 gig, 4 gig, containers,

00:47:51.170 --> 00:47:52.130
and it's fine.

00:47:52.130 --> 00:47:55.900
They just transfer over the
network a little bit slower.

00:47:55.900 --> 00:47:57.360
You had another
question, I know.

00:47:57.360 --> 00:47:59.267
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:47:59.267 --> 00:48:00.350
MARK MANDEL: There you go.

00:48:00.350 --> 00:48:00.930
Perfect.

00:48:00.930 --> 00:48:03.730
AUDIENCE: The automatic port
allocation that you showed.

00:48:03.730 --> 00:48:05.480
Can you just talk a
little bit about that?

00:48:05.480 --> 00:48:07.230
MARK MANDEL: OK, so
the question about how

00:48:07.230 --> 00:48:08.590
we do port allocation.

00:48:08.590 --> 00:48:10.270
Basically, the way
we have it set up

00:48:10.270 --> 00:48:13.510
is for each game server
it sits essentially

00:48:13.510 --> 00:48:16.190
within its own
network namespace.

00:48:16.190 --> 00:48:18.730
So what we do is the
container starts up

00:48:18.730 --> 00:48:20.410
and it's whatever
port that you tell

00:48:20.410 --> 00:48:22.202
that it's going to
start up on, so they all

00:48:22.202 --> 00:48:24.700
start up internally the same.

00:48:24.700 --> 00:48:26.760
We set up a Kubernetes
host port, basically,

00:48:26.760 --> 00:48:29.740
which what that does is it'll
set up an IP tables route that

00:48:29.740 --> 00:48:32.920
runs through the node and then
directly into the container.

00:48:32.920 --> 00:48:35.123
We found through testing
it's like worst case

00:48:35.123 --> 00:48:36.790
scenario-- [? and ?]
[INAUDIBLE] doesn't

00:48:36.790 --> 00:48:38.860
hit that very often--
about half a millisecond.

00:48:38.860 --> 00:48:41.440
But it means then you can
still run sidecar containers.

00:48:41.440 --> 00:48:43.420
You can still run other
services alongside it

00:48:43.420 --> 00:48:45.835
and know that is contained
within that network namespace,

00:48:45.835 --> 00:48:47.710
so you can do some really
nice stuff that way

00:48:47.710 --> 00:48:50.840
and it was a nice
trade off that way.

00:48:50.840 --> 00:48:54.770
I think that it was you
who had a question next.

00:48:54.770 --> 00:48:58.430
AUDIENCE: Sorry, I just think
I just missed what you're--

00:48:58.430 --> 00:49:03.100
is that free to download or
do I need to buy the trial?

00:49:03.100 --> 00:49:04.540
MARK MANDEL: For
the marketplace?

00:49:04.540 --> 00:49:05.510
Oh, that's a good question.

00:49:05.510 --> 00:49:07.010
How do I work it
on the marketplace.

00:49:07.010 --> 00:49:10.370
So if you want to install it
on Google Container Engine,

00:49:10.370 --> 00:49:14.690
we have a $300 free trial that
is valid for up to 12 months,

00:49:14.690 --> 00:49:16.430
so you can go use that.

00:49:16.430 --> 00:49:18.560
If you want to do it locally
you can do it locally.

00:49:18.560 --> 00:49:19.610
If you want to use--

00:49:19.610 --> 00:49:21.440
there's a couple of
local Kubernetes tools.

00:49:21.440 --> 00:49:24.580
Kind, K-I-N-D, or Minikube, so
you can install either of those

00:49:24.580 --> 00:49:27.080
locally and then install Agones
on that and that's not going

00:49:27.080 --> 00:49:27.980
to cost you anything.

00:49:27.980 --> 00:49:30.890
AUDIENCE: I was thinking
trying to test a multiplayer

00:49:30.890 --> 00:49:33.720
game for my own game
for just future--

00:49:33.720 --> 00:49:34.520
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:49:34.520 --> 00:49:35.420
So you could do either of those.

00:49:35.420 --> 00:49:37.128
You could test it
locally on your machine

00:49:37.128 --> 00:49:38.840
or run a trial account
on Google Cloud.

00:49:38.840 --> 00:49:41.450
AUDIENCE: I was also
thinking about that I'm

00:49:41.450 --> 00:49:43.820
attending a school at the
[INAUDIBLE] University

00:49:43.820 --> 00:49:46.523
and [? it's ?] just testing the
different computer that, hey,

00:49:46.523 --> 00:49:47.690
can you hit me or something.

00:49:47.690 --> 00:49:50.750
And then, boom, [INAUDIBLE]
you or something.

00:49:50.750 --> 00:49:52.040
[INAUDIBLE] planning for.

00:49:55.260 --> 00:49:58.950
MARK MANDEL: There was I
think someone over there.

00:49:58.950 --> 00:50:00.060
I'm staring this way.

00:50:00.060 --> 00:50:03.148
I need to turn that way next.

00:50:03.148 --> 00:50:03.690
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:50:03.690 --> 00:50:06.600
So it seems like a lot
of the architecture

00:50:06.600 --> 00:50:08.970
we just talked about
is useful in an example

00:50:08.970 --> 00:50:11.970
where you spin up a pod,
you allocate the players

00:50:11.970 --> 00:50:13.887
and it tears down in a
traditional multiplayer

00:50:13.887 --> 00:50:14.387
setting.

00:50:14.387 --> 00:50:16.110
But how does this
work where you might

00:50:16.110 --> 00:50:18.510
need interpod communication
in something like an MMO

00:50:18.510 --> 00:50:21.650
where instances are just
constantly being spinned up

00:50:21.650 --> 00:50:23.490
for different players
in different zones

00:50:23.490 --> 00:50:24.270
and that sort of thing.

00:50:24.270 --> 00:50:25.080
MARK MANDEL: That is
an excellent question.

00:50:25.080 --> 00:50:26.730
So how does this
work [INAUDIBLE]??

00:50:26.730 --> 00:50:28.620
So we have been
relatively focused

00:50:28.620 --> 00:50:31.140
on more of a
session-based thing.

00:50:31.140 --> 00:50:32.460
I definitely want to explore--

00:50:32.460 --> 00:50:34.620
or I think we definitely want
to explore, I should say.

00:50:34.620 --> 00:50:36.287
We definitely want
to explore how we can

00:50:36.287 --> 00:50:38.100
support more MMO type stuff.

00:50:38.100 --> 00:50:40.698
The nice thing about
Kubernetes is the way

00:50:40.698 --> 00:50:42.240
it's set up with
its networking model

00:50:42.240 --> 00:50:46.005
from a foundation is that all
pods can talk to each other.

00:50:46.005 --> 00:50:47.880
As long as you have the
IP address of the pod

00:50:47.880 --> 00:50:49.422
you know you can
actually communicate

00:50:49.422 --> 00:50:51.690
within the networking
system within the cluster,

00:50:51.690 --> 00:50:53.440
so that sort of stuff
is available to you.

00:50:53.440 --> 00:50:57.190
But I actually do want to
explore more if there are

00:50:57.190 --> 00:51:00.400
things that we can do at more of
that foundational orchestration

00:51:00.400 --> 00:51:03.490
layer of how can we-- and I
don't know exactly what that

00:51:03.490 --> 00:51:04.930
looks like, but how can we do--

00:51:04.930 --> 00:51:07.870
help you with geographic
splitting or that kind stuff.

00:51:07.870 --> 00:51:10.390
That being said, there's nothing
that necessarily stops you

00:51:10.390 --> 00:51:11.080
from doing that.

00:51:11.080 --> 00:51:14.053
You can leave these up for as
long as you like if you want.

00:51:14.053 --> 00:51:15.720
They don't necessarily
have to come down

00:51:15.720 --> 00:51:17.470
in three minutes or
20 minutes or an hour.

00:51:17.470 --> 00:51:19.637
As long as you want to do
a little bit of extra work

00:51:19.637 --> 00:51:22.190
over the top you can still use
them for doing MMO type stuff.

00:51:22.190 --> 00:51:24.920
There's nothing inherently there
that stops you from doing that.

00:51:24.920 --> 00:51:26.440
AUDIENCE: So I have one more.

00:51:26.440 --> 00:51:30.248
So you talked a little bit
about hosting on Windows.

00:51:30.248 --> 00:51:31.165
MARK MANDEL: Oh, yeah.

00:51:31.165 --> 00:51:33.460
AUDIENCE: I was just curious
about how much exploration

00:51:33.460 --> 00:51:35.332
you've done in
terms of nodes that

00:51:35.332 --> 00:51:36.790
are Windows in
Kubernetes clusters,

00:51:36.790 --> 00:51:40.180
but then more importantly
Windows containers.

00:51:40.180 --> 00:51:42.788
Specifically because we work
on the tools for PlayStation

00:51:42.788 --> 00:51:43.330
development--

00:51:43.330 --> 00:51:43.930
MARK MANDEL: Got it.

00:51:43.930 --> 00:51:45.638
AUDIENCE: --where most
of it happens on--

00:51:45.638 --> 00:51:48.140
MARK MANDEL: I have not
explored that really at all.

00:51:48.140 --> 00:51:51.730
There is a lot of work that is
happening with running Windows

00:51:51.730 --> 00:51:54.160
containers inside Kubernetes.

00:51:54.160 --> 00:51:55.600
I'm not an expert on that area.

00:51:55.600 --> 00:51:56.440
There's a whole SIG for it.

00:51:56.440 --> 00:51:57.280
There's a whole
special interest group

00:51:57.280 --> 00:51:58.930
for Windows and containers.

00:51:58.930 --> 00:52:00.670
As far as I understand
it, it is coming.

00:52:00.670 --> 00:52:02.170
They're working on it.

00:52:02.170 --> 00:52:04.630
It was required-- there
was some requirement

00:52:04.630 --> 00:52:06.997
for some early release
type stuff on Windows

00:52:06.997 --> 00:52:08.830
to get the Windows
containers support needed

00:52:08.830 --> 00:52:10.760
for Kubernetes [INAUDIBLE]
coming through,

00:52:10.760 --> 00:52:11.620
but it is coming.

00:52:11.620 --> 00:52:12.920
It's just a question
of when it gets done.

00:52:12.920 --> 00:52:14.860
AUDIENCE: Is the answer just
go talk to Microsoft guys?

00:52:14.860 --> 00:52:15.370
MARK MANDEL: Sorry?

00:52:15.370 --> 00:52:17.560
AUDIENCE: Is the answer just
go talk to the Microsoft guys?

00:52:17.560 --> 00:52:18.640
MARK MANDEL: Go join--

00:52:18.640 --> 00:52:22.000
there's a SIG Windows
group in Kubernetes.

00:52:22.000 --> 00:52:25.335
The way the actual open-source
project is structured

00:52:25.335 --> 00:52:27.710
is they have special interest
groups for different areas.

00:52:27.710 --> 00:52:29.560
So you can go on Slack, you
can go on the mailing lists.

00:52:29.560 --> 00:52:30.590
They have regular meetings.

00:52:30.590 --> 00:52:32.923
So basically, you can go see
what the structure is there

00:52:32.923 --> 00:52:34.450
and everything is available.

00:52:34.450 --> 00:52:35.120
It's all open.

00:52:35.120 --> 00:52:36.037
AUDIENCE: OK, awesome.

00:52:36.037 --> 00:52:36.610
Thank you.

00:52:36.610 --> 00:52:37.894
MARK MANDEL: It was over here.

00:52:37.894 --> 00:52:39.352
I've been facing
on the other side.

00:52:43.080 --> 00:52:45.093
AUDIENCE: How does this
fit in with Stadia?

00:52:45.093 --> 00:52:47.010
MARK MANDEL: How does
this fit in with Stadia?

00:52:47.010 --> 00:52:50.440
Actually, that took longer
than I thought it would to ask.

00:52:50.440 --> 00:52:53.642
The short answer here
I think is Stadia's--

00:52:53.642 --> 00:52:55.350
I'm not going to talk
on behalf of Stadia

00:52:55.350 --> 00:52:56.850
because that's not
the product area.

00:52:56.850 --> 00:53:00.600
But the short answer
here is we talk a bit

00:53:00.600 --> 00:53:03.880
about our network essentially.

00:53:03.880 --> 00:53:06.450
And so if there are instances
which you are using Stadia

00:53:06.450 --> 00:53:08.790
and you want to have a dedicated
game server, running it

00:53:08.790 --> 00:53:10.920
inside Google's network
is ideal because we

00:53:10.920 --> 00:53:12.510
don't have to go outside.

00:53:12.510 --> 00:53:14.030
We're inside our own network.

00:53:14.030 --> 00:53:15.780
Our product manager
was talking about this

00:53:15.780 --> 00:53:16.720
earlier this morning.

00:53:16.720 --> 00:53:18.720
There's a lot of inherent
speed and cost savings

00:53:18.720 --> 00:53:19.428
you can do there.

00:53:19.428 --> 00:53:21.030
So there are some
instances where

00:53:21.030 --> 00:53:23.520
you may want to run a
dedicated game server,

00:53:23.520 --> 00:53:26.910
but we allow you to do
that and so awesome.

00:53:26.910 --> 00:53:30.460
That's the short answer.

00:53:30.460 --> 00:53:33.303
I figured I would get
that question like first.

00:53:33.303 --> 00:53:34.470
We have a couple of minutes.

00:53:34.470 --> 00:53:35.823
Someone over there.

00:53:38.258 --> 00:53:39.800
AUDIENCE: Thanks
for giving the talk.

00:53:39.800 --> 00:53:42.460
You mentioned getting some
of the metrics out of Agones,

00:53:42.460 --> 00:53:45.850
but what I'm more
curious about is managing

00:53:45.850 --> 00:53:47.180
the complexity of Kubernetes.

00:53:47.180 --> 00:53:49.970
Are there any good
tools for pulling out

00:53:49.970 --> 00:53:51.700
stack traces of server
crashes or things

00:53:51.700 --> 00:53:54.470
like that that you
know of presently?

00:53:54.470 --> 00:53:56.380
MARK MANDEL: So
generally speaking,

00:53:56.380 --> 00:53:58.570
as long as anything goes--

00:53:58.570 --> 00:54:02.450
any text goes-- actually,
here's an interesting question.

00:54:02.450 --> 00:54:04.120
How do you dump your
server traces now?

00:54:04.120 --> 00:54:06.662
Is that just straight to a text
file, does it commit to logs?

00:54:06.662 --> 00:54:08.745
Are you doing heap dumps?

00:54:08.745 --> 00:54:10.870
AUDIENCE: Logs that are
then [INAUDIBLE] external--

00:54:10.870 --> 00:54:12.690
MARK MANDEL: As
long as any log--

00:54:12.690 --> 00:54:16.450
so speaking of-- let me
try this sentence again.

00:54:16.450 --> 00:54:18.790
Speaking for Google
Kubernetes Engines,

00:54:18.790 --> 00:54:21.730
and generally speaking
on Kubernetes as a whole,

00:54:21.730 --> 00:54:24.430
if you spit your logs
out to foreground--

00:54:24.430 --> 00:54:25.960
so basically to standard out--

00:54:25.960 --> 00:54:27.460
then they get
aggregated [INAUDIBLE]

00:54:27.460 --> 00:54:28.690
central logging service.

00:54:28.690 --> 00:54:31.115
If you're running on
Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:54:31.115 --> 00:54:33.490
it's already set up so that
our Google Container Engine--

00:54:33.490 --> 00:54:34.730
sorry, that was the old name.

00:54:34.730 --> 00:54:36.310
It's already set up
so that all the logs

00:54:36.310 --> 00:54:37.840
that go to [? Foreground ?]
go to Stackdriver.

00:54:37.840 --> 00:54:38.632
They're searchable.

00:54:38.632 --> 00:54:40.132
You can output stuff.

00:54:40.132 --> 00:54:41.590
So that's all
available to you just

00:54:41.590 --> 00:54:43.360
as standard log aggregation.

00:54:43.360 --> 00:54:45.190
And like I said in
the talk, standing

00:54:45.190 --> 00:54:46.330
on the shoulders of giants.

00:54:46.330 --> 00:54:47.530
That stuff we didn't have to do.

00:54:47.530 --> 00:54:48.220
It's just great.

00:54:48.220 --> 00:54:49.310
It's just there.

00:54:49.310 --> 00:54:50.590
It's awesome.

00:54:50.590 --> 00:54:54.495
And even in Agones itself we
actually spit out JSON logs.

00:54:54.495 --> 00:54:56.620
So we do structured logging,
and that goes straight

00:54:56.620 --> 00:54:57.970
into Stackdriver and
it's like, oh, it's JSON.

00:54:57.970 --> 00:54:58.470
Awesome.

00:54:58.470 --> 00:55:00.440
And now it's all searchable
and it's fantastic.

00:55:00.440 --> 00:55:01.400
So I'm super happy.

00:55:01.400 --> 00:55:02.830
It's not work we have to do.

00:55:02.830 --> 00:55:05.420
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

